{"id":227315,"title":"SA/25/2023 of AASHI MANDOLIYA Vs SADANAND MANDOLIYA","court":"Nainital HC","case_no":"SA/25/2023","cite":null,"report":null,"date":"2023-03-27","judges":null,"disposal":"ALLOWED","nateeja":"Paksh mein","pages":4,"hits":{"Is case ke shabd":[0]},"paras":[{"seq":0,"no":null,"section":"body","text":"SL.\nNo Date\nOffice Notes, reports,\norders or proceedings or\ndirections and Registrar's\norder with Signatures\nCOURT'S OR JUDGES'S ORDERS\nSA No.25 of 2023\nHon'ble Sharad Kumar Sharma, J.\nMr. Vikas Bahuguna, learned\ncounsel for the appellant.\nMr. Sagar Kothari, learned counsel\nfor the caveator.\nThis second appeal is being heard\nfinally with the consent of the parties, on\nits own merits for its final adjudication.\nAdmit the appeal.\nSince the parties have agreed to\naddress the appeal on merit, the same is\nbeing heard finally.\nThe plaintiff to Suit No.1 of 2018 had\ninstituted a suit for the grant of decree of\npermanent and mandatory injunction, as\nagainst the defendant herein and,\nparticularly, defendant no.2, who is the\ndaughter-in-law of the plaintiff, whose\nrights would obviously flow from\nwhatsoever right is vested on her husband\nMr. Ramesh Chand Mandoliya, who has\nnot been chosen as to be made as a\ndefendant party to the suit in question.\nThe following reliefs were sought:-\n\"क - यह �क ्ቚ�तवाद�गण 1 से 3 तथा"},{"seq":1,"no":null,"section":"body","text":"उनके एजेन्ट� , नौकरो ्ቚ�त�न�धयो\nउ�रा�धकार�यो को स्थाई व्यादेश क� आ���\n�ारा �न�ष� �कया जाये �क ्ቚ�तवाद�गण 1\nसे 2 इस वादप्ቔ क� सूची म� विणर्त सम्प��\nके स्वरुप को न बदले और न ह� सम्प�� पर\nतृतीय प� के अ�धकार सृिजत करे और न\nह� सम्प�� को ्ቚत्य� अ्ቚत्य� रूप से\n�व्ቅ�त अंत�रत व हस्तांत�रत तथा\nअ�त्ቅमण न करे और न ह� इसका कारण\nबने।\nख- यह �क ्ቚ�तवाद�गण को आ�ात्मक\nव्यादेश �ारा आदे�शत �कया जाये �क\nवाद्ቇस्त सम्प�� से अपना अवैध �नमार्ण\nहटा ले तथा वाद्ቇस्त सम्प�� पर हस्ता�ेप\nन करे।\""},{"seq":2,"no":null,"section":"body","text":"However, the learned trial Court has\nproceeded ex-parte and has dismissed the\nsuit by the judgment of 02.03.2019. When\na trial Court proceeds to dismiss the suit\neven if by an ex-parte decree, it is not that\nthe trial Court is eradicated of its\nresponsibility to venture into the evidence\nof the matter and to consider the propriety\nof the right of the plaintiff in relation to\nthe suit for which the relief has been\nsought.\n But this Court at this stage, is not\nremarking upon the judgment of the trial\nCourt, as it was rendered on 02.03.2019"},{"seq":3,"no":null,"section":"body","text":"as, the said judgment under the principle\nof merger has vested into judgment\nrendered in Civil Appeal No.28 of 2019,\n\"Sadanand Mandoliya Vs. Suresh\nBarthwal and Others\" where the First\nAppellate Court vide its impugned\njudgment of 23.11.2022 had allowed the\nappeal and has set aside the ex-parte\ndecree dated 02.03.2019. Even if the\nAppellate Court's judgement dated\n23.11.2022 is taken into consideration,\napart from making reference to the issues\nwhich were framed by the trial Court in\nparagraph no.12, the Appellate Court has\nnot applied it's mind at all, rather, has\nborrowed the issues to satisfy the\nimplications of the provisions contained\nunder Order 41 Rule 31 of CPC and, apart\nfrom it, since the Appellate Court being\nthe first Court of Appeal is the next\nsuperior Court, which could exercise its\nrights of appreciation of evidence for\ndetermining the rights of a party in a lis.\nThe Appellate Court's judgment does\nnot satisfy the test of the parameters laid\ndown by the judgment of the Hon'ble Apex\nCourt as reported in AIR 1983 SC 114\nwherein it provides that since the\nAppellate Court being the first superior"},{"seq":4,"no":null,"section":"body","text":"Court of Appeal, it has to apply its mind\nand record its finding by laying down the\npoints of determination and findings\nthereof.\nSince having not done so, this Court\nis of the view that the second appeal\ndeserves to be allowed. The second appeal,\naccordingly, stands allowed. As a\nconsequence thereto, the judgment of the\nfirst Appellate Court is set aside and the\nmatter is remitted back to the learned\nFirst Appellate Court to reconsider the\nissue and decide the same after\nformulation of points of determination\nunder Order 41 Rule 31 and then decide\nthe lis on its own merits.\nSubject to the aforesaid, the matter\nis remitted back to the learned first\nAppellate Court to decide the matter\nafresh."},{"seq":5,"no":null,"section":"body","text":"(Sharad Kumar Sharma, J.)\n 27.03.2023\nSukhbant"}]}